module lib/optim
  Optimizers for a model whose parameters are one flat vector with a layout
  (lib/params): plain gradient descent, momentum, Adam and Muon, one registry
  of their steps, and the contract every one of them is tested against. And
  Adam and Muon for a model held as records of matrices, one matrix at a time,
  applied over the model with `for_leaves`; a test holds them to the flat
  versions bit for bit.

optimizer  @e6ec446f
  what  A settings vector: which optimizer, and its constants.

sgd  @77b000d6
  what  Plain gradient descent at step size `lr`.

momentum  @1ea6fee8
  what  Gradient descent along a running sum of gradients that decays by `mu` a step.

adam  @0232c997
  what  Adam, with the decay rates of its two running averages.

muon  @c83819fb
  what  Muon, with the decay rate of its momentum.

optim_state  @3c184e5c
  what  The memory an optimizer carries between steps: room for two vectors of the
        model's layout, the first moment and then the second.

sgd_step  @764522a9
  what  `theta <- theta - lr g`.

momentum_step  @fb69fb45
  what  `m <- mu m + g`, then `theta <- theta - lr m`. The momentum is the first vector
        of the state.

adam_step  @2edd2653
  what  Adam. Both averages start at zero, so early on they are too small by a factor `1
        - beta^t`; dividing that out is the bias correction. `eps` keeps a coordinate
        whose gradient has always been zero from dividing by zero.
  opt     the settings vector
  theta   the parameters, changed in place
  grad    their gradient
  state   the first moment, then the second
  layout  the parameters' layout, which Adam does not need
  beta1   the first moment's decay rate
  beta2   the second moment's decay rate
  n       the number of parameters
  mean    the first moment, bias corrected
  spread  the root of the second moment, bias corrected

muon_matrix_direction  @c15ef97e
  what  The direction Muon steps along for one momentum matrix. The Newton-Schulz
        iteration of `lib/polar` wants at least as many columns as rows, because it
        multiplies by `X X^T` and that is the smaller product then; a tall matrix
        goes through transposed and comes back.
  M     the momentum matrix

muon_step  @6b766902
  what  Muon: momentum over the whole vector, then, one tensor at a time, step along the
        orthogonalized momentum of that tensor. A tensor with one row or one column has
        one singular value, so for it this is a step of fixed length along the momentum.
  opt        the settings vector
  theta      the parameters, changed in place
  grad       their gradient
  state      the momentum, in its first half
  layout     the parameters' layout: which numbers make up each matrix
  n          the number of parameters
  k          the tensor being stepped
  direction  its orthogonalized momentum

adam_update  @002ab764
  what      Adam's update of one matrix: running averages of each entry's
            gradient and squared gradient, corrected for starting at zero, and a
            step by their ratio. `for_leaves(adam_update, model, grad, m, v,
            taken, lr_scale, settings)` updates every matrix of a model held as
            records.
  why       Each entry moves at about the rate `lr` whatever the size of its
            gradient, which suits a table whose rows are used unevenly, such as
            an embedding.
  w         the matrix, changed in place
  g         its gradient
  m         the running average of the gradient, changed in place
  v         the running average of the squared gradient, changed in place
  taken     how many updates have been made, this one included; the bias
            correction needs it
  lr_scale  the fraction of the starting step size a schedule allows now
  s         the step size, the two decay rates and the epsilon
  mean      the average gradient, bias corrected
  spread    the root of the average squared gradient, bias corrected

  $$ \theta \gets \theta - \eta \, \frac{\hat m}{\sqrt{\hat v} + \epsilon} $$

muon_update  @dbd6197c
  what      Muon's update of one matrix: keep a momentum, replace it by a
            matrix with the same singular vectors and singular values near $1$
            (`muon_matrix_direction`), and step along that, scaled by
            $\sqrt{\max(1, \text{rows}/\text{cols})}$ as the nanogpt speedrun
            does.
  why       The step has the same size in every direction of the matrix however
            lopsided the gradient is. For a matrix at least as wide as it is
            tall the scale is $1$ and this is the flat `muon_step`.
  w         the matrix, changed in place
  g         $G$  its gradient
  mom       $M$  its momentum, changed in place: $M \gets \mu M + G$
  lr_scale  the fraction of the starting step size a schedule allows now
  s         the step size and the momentum's decay rate
  rows      the matrix's rows
  cols      its columns
  size      the step length for this matrix

  $$ M \gets \mu M + G, \qquad W \gets W - \eta \sqrt{\max(1, r/c)}\; \mathrm{NS}_5(M) $$

optimizers  @3333f1c2
  what  Every optimizer, indexed by `SGD`, `MOMENTUM`, `ADAM`, `MUON`.

optimizer_count  @47a5c6bb
  what  How many optimizers the registry holds.

optim_step  @b9a6d5f5
  what  One step of whichever optimizer `opt` describes: count it, then move `theta`
        using `grad` and the optimizer's memory in `state`.

optimizer_of  @c9e638c4
  what  Optimizer `kind` with the constants it usually runs at: every momentum decays by
        `0.9` a step, and Adam's second average by `0.999`. The step size starts at zero,
        because a schedule sets it before every step.

decayed  @e92e5b19
  what  The step-size schedule the contract runs every optimizer on: `lr` at the start,
        falling in a straight line toward zero, which it would reach one step after
        the last. Muon's step has a length it chose, not the gradient's, so without
        the decay it circles the minimum at a distance of about `lr`.

quadratic_layout  @a5b139ab
  what  A convex quadratic over two tensors, a `3 x 5` matrix and a `1 x 4` row: `f = 1/2
        sum_i h_i (theta_i - c_i)^2`, with curvatures `h_i` between `1` and `4`. Its minimum
        is `theta = c`, where `f = 0`.

quadratic_curvature  @d17c2718
  what  The curvature of coordinate `i` of the quadratic.

quadratic_center  @ae1eb58e
  what  The minimizer of the quadratic.

quadratic_gradient  @ce165c4c
  what  The quadratic's gradient, `h_i (theta_i - c_i)`, written into `grad`.

quadratic_miss  @76cd88ab
  what  Run `opt` on the quadratic from `theta = 0` for `steps` steps and return the
        largest distance of any coordinate from its minimizer.

lstsq_inputs  @3f782fd9
  what  A least-squares problem: find the `4 x 3` matrix `W` that makes `X W` closest to
        `Y`, with `X` `8 x 4`, measured by `f = |X W - Y|^2 / (2 * 8)`. Eight equations
        per column and four unknowns, so no `W` fits exactly.

lstsq_targets  @24b3787f
  what  The targets `Y`, `8 x 3`.

lstsq_layout  @23863ae6
  what  The layout of the unknown: one `4 x 3` matrix, taller than it is wide, so Muon
        has to take it through transposed.

lstsq_solution  @ca262af7
  what  The exact minimizer, from the normal equations `X^T X W = X^T Y`, all three
        columns in one solve.

lstsq_gradient  @5537cf39
  what  The gradient of the least-squares loss, `X^T (X W - Y) / 8`.

lstsq_miss  @a6f2a073
  what  Run `opt` on the least-squares problem from `W = 0` and return the largest
        distance of any entry of `W` from the exact minimizer.

contract_lr  @7cb3914e
  what  The starting step size each optimizer runs the contract at, indexed by kind
        (SGD, momentum, Adam, Muon).

optim_case  @9e781a6b
  what  Two wide matrices, `2 x 5` and `3 x 4`, cut from a test matrix: a model
        held as a record, for the test that compares the record optimizers with
        the flat ones.

optim_case_layout  @f01d9a34
  what  The layout of the same two matrices in one vector.

optim_case_flat  @c8d22cab
  what  The record's two matrices written into one vector of that layout.
